intro='''<section class="studio-hero"><div><span class="kicker">Y9 ELECTRICITY · BUILD, MEASURE AND EXPLAIN</span><h1>Your circuit.<br>Your investigation.</h1><p>Drag the equipment onto the bench, join the sockets and use the instruments. Follow the four investigations from your circuit activity, or build your own network in Free construction.</p><div class="row"><a class="button primary" href="#construction">Start building ↓</a><a class="button" href="../circuits/#learn-first">Read the circuit lesson →</a></div></div><img src="../images/construction-studio.svg" alt="A shaded DC supply, ammeter and resistor wired into a circuit" width="1000" height="600"></section>
<section class="studio-reading" aria-label="Understand the equipment"><article><h3>A circuit needs a path.</h3><p>A direct-current (DC) supply maintains a <strong>potential difference</strong>, or voltage, between its terminals. Wires provide conducting connections; a resistor is a component that opposes current. Potential difference, often shortened to p.d., means energy transferred per unit charge: 1 V = 1 J/C, one joule per coulomb. A joule (J) measures energy; a coulomb (C) measures charge. A <strong>junction</strong> is where paths meet. In this model, wires crossing on the drawing connect only at sockets.</p><p class="formula"><strong>I = V/R</strong>I: current in amperes (A)<br>V: potential difference in volts (V)<br>R: resistance in ohms (Ω)<br>9 V ÷ 10 Ω = 0.90 A</p></article><article><h3>Measure through and across.</h3><p>An <strong>ammeter</strong> measures current through its path, so insert it <strong>in series</strong>. A <strong>voltmeter</strong> compares two points, so place its probes <strong>across</strong> a resistor. It reports the red probe’s potential minus the black probe’s potential; a minus sign can mean the probes are reversed. Conventional current is defined from positive to negative outside the supply. Negative electrons in a metal drift the other way; the bench can show both views.</p><p class="memory">A is Along the path; V Views two points. A current of 1 A means 1 C passes a point each second. Components transfer energy while charge continues around a steady loop.</p></article><article><h3>Make a fair comparison.</h3><p>Change one factor while holding the others fixed. These resistors stay at constant temperature, so their resistance stays fixed until you adjust it. The ideal supply and meters let you investigate patterns without lead resistance or measurement noise. <strong>Power</strong> is the rate of energy transfer: P = VI, where P is measured in watts (W). One watt means one joule per second. A 12 V load carrying 0.60 A transfers energy at 7.2 W.</p><p class="word-key"><strong>Two modes:</strong> Guide me highlights one practical action at a time. Free construction lets you arrange and connect one supply with any supported network of loads, switches, junctions and meters.</p></article></section>'''
bench='''<section id="construction"><div class="section-head"><span class="section-number">01</span><div><span class="kicker">PREDICT · BUILD · MEASURE</span><h2>Construct it on the workbench.</h2><p>Drag a card to place equipment, or click it for a suggested position. Operate controls on the instruments.</p></div></div><div class="construction-shell" id="construction-shell"><div class="studio-toolbar"><div class="mode-switch" role="group" aria-label="Construction mode"><button id="mode-guided" aria-pressed="true">Guide me</button><button id="mode-free" aria-pressed="false">Free construction</button></div><label id="investigation-label">Choose an investigation<select id="investigation"></select></label><div class="row"><button id="undo-build" disabled>Undo</button><button id="pause-build" aria-pressed="false">Pause motion</button><button id="clear-build">Clear workbench</button></div></div><div class="studio-instruction"><span id="build-instruction">Choose a material and start your circuit.</span><button id="help-build">Help me complete this step</button></div><div class="studio-grid"><aside class="tool-shelf" id="tool-shelf" aria-label="Equipment shelf"><p>DRAG OR CLICK</p><div id="equipment-tools"></div><label class="tiny-key" for="flow-display">Show motion<select id="flow-display"><option value="both">Both views</option><option value="current">Current</option><option value="electrons">Electrons</option><option value="none">Hide motion</option></select></label><label class="tiny-key"><input id="show-values" type="checkbox" checked> Show values</label><p class="flow-key"><span class="electron-key">●</span> Electrons<br><span class="current-key">➜</span> Current</p></aside><div class="bench-stage" id="bench-stage"><div id="board-host"></div><div class="selection-dock" id="selection-dock" hidden><span id="selection-name"></span><button id="edit-selected">Set value</button><button id="rotate-selected">Rotate</button><button id="delete-selected">Remove</button></div><div class="instrument-editor" id="instrument-editor" hidden><h3 id="editor-title"></h3><label id="editor-label" for="editor-range"></label><input id="editor-range" type="range"><input id="editor-number" type="number" aria-label="Exact instrument setting"><div class="row"><button id="apply-setting" class="primary">Apply setting</button><button id="close-editor">Close</button></div><p id="editor-note"></p></div></div><aside class="studio-observations"><span class="kicker">YOUR MEASUREMENTS</span><h2 id="observation-title">One setting at a time.</h2><p class="mode-status" id="network-state"></p><div class="guide-progress" id="guide-progress"></div><div class="live-summary"><div>Supply setting<b id="studio-voltage">—</b></div><div>Supply current<b id="studio-current">—</b></div></div><p id="meter-guide"></p><div class="measure-chips" id="measurement-chips"></div><p class="studio-graph-title" id="studio-graph-title">Your recorded pattern</p><div class="studio-graph" id="studio-graph"></div><p class="tiny-key" id="studio-graph-key"></p><p id="build-feedback" class="status" role="status"></p><label class="tiny-key" id="example-label" hidden>Load a worked example<select id="worked-example"><option value="">Choose an example…</option value="resistance">9 V · one resistor</option><option value="series">12 V · series</option><option value="parallel">12 V · parallel</option></select></label></aside></div><div class="studio-help-line"><span>Drag a wire from one socket to another, or click two sockets. Drag coloured probe handles onto sockets.</span><details><summary>Keyboard and editing help</summary><ul><li>Click a material card, or press Enter on it, to add equipment.</li><li>Focus a part and use arrow keys to move it. Press R to rotate and Delete to remove it.</li><li>Double-click a supply, resistor or model lamp, or use its on-instrument adjustment button.</li><li>Press Enter on a socket and then on another socket to connect them. Select a probe first to attach it instead.</li><li>Click a wire and choose Remove, or press Delete while it is focused. Undo restores the previous change.</li></ul></details></div><div class="evidence-panel"><div class="notebook-tools"><div><span class="kicker">YOUR EVIDENCE</span><h2>Record and compare your results.</h2></div><div><button class="primary" id="studio-record">Record this reading</button><button id="studio-clear-readings">Clear this table</button><button id="studio-export">Download CSV</button></div></div><p id="table-instruction"></p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead id="studio-table-head"></thead><tbody id="studio-table-body"></tbody></table></div><p id="notebook-feedback" class="status" role="status"></p><p>These practical readings and concept checks stay on this page. For a recorded Test yourself quiz, use the separate revision simulator.</p></div></div></section>'''
after='''<section id="investigation-notes" class="experiment-intro"><div><span class="kicker">UNDERSTAND THE INVESTIGATION</span><h2 id="stage-title"></h2><p id="stage-intro"></p></div><div class="formula" id="stage-theory"></div></section><section id="studio-concepts"><div class="section-head"><span class="section-number">02</span><div><span class="kicker">CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING</span><h2>Explain the pattern you measured.</h2><p>Choose an answer, compare the explanation and try again where needed.</p></div></div><div id="studio-questions" class="concept-grid"></div><div class="next-investigation"><p id="stage-completion"></p><button id="next-investigation">Next investigation →</button></div></section><details class="sources"><summary>Worksheet source and model details</summary><p>Original Learning Lab construction activity following the teacher-provided <cite>Circuits Simulation.docx</cite>: resistance at 9 V, voltage with 10 Ω, then series and parallel investigations at 12 V with R₁ = 10 Ω and R₂ = 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 and 60 Ω. The document’s screenshots were used to understand the circuits; this workbench and its equipment artwork are original and run independently of the linked PhET simulation.</p><p>One ideal regulated DC supply, ideal wires, ideal in-series ammeters and non-loading voltmeters. Loads have constant resistance at controlled temperature, including the explicitly labelled model lamp. Protective supply states interrupt a short or a demand above 5 A. The free mode solves the connections you build, including mixed networks; it does not classify every circuit as a preset. Wire-loop current distribution can be indeterminate with ideal wires, so its motion is qualitative. Particle sizes, counts and speeds are illustrative.</p></details><section class="revision-invite" id="revision-invitation"><div><span class="kicker">03 / INDEPENDENT REVISION</span><h2>Ready to test your understanding?</h2><p>Choose topics and levels in the circuits revision simulator. Practice gives explanations; Test yourself checks your independent understanding.</p></div><a class="button primary" href="../circuits/revision.html">Open circuits revision →</a></section>'''
